  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1)\n\nThe overall forecast and impacts from the remnants of Hurricane \nIsaias remain on track for the Ohio Valley. \n\nSurface winds will increase Saturday afternoon as the system\napproaches. Sustained winds out of the SE will increase to around\n12-18 MPH, with gusts of 20-30 MPH. Winds will continue to increase \nthroughout the evening and are expected to peak during the late \nevening and early morning hours Sunday, with gusts generally between\n25-35 MPH. Cannot rule out some isolated gusts up to 40 MPH, but \nguidance suggests this would be a low probability. \n\nRain may begin as early as the late afternoon hours on Saturday,\nmainly south of the Ohio River. The rain will initially be light at \nthe onset, but coverage/intensity of showers will increase as we \nprogress into the overnight hours. Some embedded thunderstorms remain\npossible as instability builds into the region. Still not \nanticipating any severe threat - the recent model runs have pulled \nthe center of the low slightly more to the SE, which would favor a \nlower severe threat. Will continue to monitor trends with the \nremnants as they track inland. The heaviest rainfall is expected \nSaturday night into Sunday morning. Showers become more scattered by \nSunday afternoon, with the rain gradually tapering off from west to \neast as the system tracks NE. Total QPF still appears to be around \n0.75\" to 1.5\" for the majority of our counties. CAMs are beginning \nto show heavier rain bands that could produce some locally higher \nrainfall amounts of 2\" or greater, with this primarily focused \nsomewhere along/SE of I-71. Still not expecting a flash flood threat \nbased on rainfall amounts and the fact that the rain will fall over \nan extended period of time. Still could have some nuisance flooding \nwhere the heavier rain bands form.\n\nKey Message 2)\n\nH5 flow pattern becomes very meridional next week, which will result\nin a more active pattern. Temperatures to start the work work will be\n5-10 degrees above normal. A weak system and associated cold front\nwill swing through Tuesday into Tuesday night, which could result in\nsome spotty showers. The front will stall out south of the Ohio \nRiver on Wednesday, with another low pressure system developing out \nin the Central Plains. This will result in additional precipitation\nchances Wednesday into Thursday.",
